{% set local_problem %}
<p>Scottsboro's proximity to Lake Guntersville creates a humid environment that's especially hard on exposed wood. Fascia boards — the wood trim where gutters attach — bear the brunt of this exposure. When gutters leak or overflow, water soaks into fascia that already struggles to dry out in the humid air.</p>

<p>We see more fascia rot per capita in the Scottsboro area than in drier parts of our service region. Lake houses and vacation properties are particularly vulnerable because small problems can go unnoticed while owners are away, giving rot time to spread.</p>

<p>Older downtown Scottsboro homes face a different challenge: decades of accumulated wear on original wood trim. Many of these homes have fascia that's been painted over multiple times, hiding damage beneath the surface.</p>

<p>Whatever the cause, installing new gutters on damaged fascia is pointless — they won't stay attached properly. We inspect fascia on every job and repair what's needed first.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We start with a thorough inspection, checking fascia (and soffit if needed) for water damage, soft spots, and rot. We'll show you exactly what we find and explain what needs repair.</p>

<p>For damaged sections, we carefully remove existing gutters, cut out the rotted wood, and install new fascia boards. We use quality primed lumber or composite materials designed for exterior exposure in humid climates.</p>

<p>Then we install your new gutters on fresh, solid fascia — properly attached, properly sloped, and designed to prevent the overflow that caused the problem in the first place.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia in Jackson County for over 20 years. We understand how Scottsboro's humidity accelerates wood damage and what materials and techniques work best in this environment.</p>

<p>For vacation homeowners, we offer inspection and documentation services so you know exactly what's happening with your property — even when you can't be there yourself.</p>
{% endset %}
